%T A401748 3,0,1,1,2,4,0,1,1,3,1,0,1,2,1,2,0,1,2,1,4,0,1,2,3,1,0,1,2,3,4,0,1,1,
%U A401748 2,1,2,0,1,1,2,1,3,0,1,1,2,1,4,0,1,1,2,1,5
%N A401748 Finite sequences satisfying the A400000 visibility criterion, sorted first by length then lexicographically.
%C A401748 A sequence is valid if each building of height h sees exactly h earlier buildings. By removing the A400000 requirement for each building to be as short as possible we get more than 1 solution for 3 or more buildings.
%C A401748 Number of sequences and therefore rows of length k is A401749.
%C A401748 Each solution of length > 1 starts with buildings of height 0 and 1.
%H A401748 Žiga Pirc, <a href="/A401748/b401748.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..17262 (rows of length 1..11)</a>
%H A401748 Žiga Pirc, <a href="/A401748/a401748.cpp.txt">C++ program used for the b-file</a>
%e A401748 Table starts:
%e A401748   0,
%e A401748   0, 1,
%e A401748   0, 1, 1,
%e A401748   0, 1, 2,
%e A401748   0, 1, 1, 2,
%e A401748   0, 1, 1, 3,
%e A401748   0, 1, 2, 1,
%e A401748   0, 1, 2, 3,
%e A401748   0, 1, 1, 2, 1,
%e A401748   0, 1, 1, 2, 3,
%e A401748   0, 1, 1, 2, 4,
%e A401748   0, 1, 1, 3, 1,
%e A401748   0, 1, 2, 1, 2,
%e A401748   0, 1, 2, 1, 4,
%e A401748   0, 1, 2, 3, 1,
%e A401748   0, 1, 2, 3, 4,
%e A401748 ...
%e A401748 Row 9 gives the lexicographically smallest sequence of length 5, so that is also the first 5 terms of A400000.
%e A401748 0, 1, 2, 1, 4 is a valid sequence since the first building sees 0 buildings, second sees the first, third sees both of them, fourth only sees the third and fifth sees all of them.
